He was a polymath, so besides art, he did scientific experiments. He studied anatomy with dissections, wrote notebooks with sketches and observations. His notebooks are called the "Codex Exposita," which include diagrams and notes on various subjects. He worked on engineering projects, like bridges, flying machines, and military weapons. He designed a helicopter prototype, though he didn't build it.

In terms of legacy, he's considered one of the greatest artists of all time. His work influenced later artists and scientists. He was also patroned by rulers, like Philip V of Florence, who commissioned many of his works.

Wait, should I mention his death? He died in 1519, possibly due to illness or suicide. Some accounts say he was troubled by financial issues after his wife Maria became ill, but others suggest he committed suicide.

